---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_ii4_109",
  "series": "Band II 4 (1701 - 1707)",
  "seq": 109,
  "text": "<p> [[331.3]] <b>109. DAMARIS LADY MASHAM AN LEIBNIZ</p>\n<p> [[331.4]] Oates, 20. (31.) Oktober 1705. [105.111.] </b></p>\n<p> [[331.15]] S^#'r Oates 20 October 1705. </p>\n<p> [[331.16]] I should have no Cause to wonder if you were not unwilling to Give over a commerce\n[[331.17]] which brought you so poor returns as are my Letters for Yours. Your Excuseing to me therefore\n[[331.18]] your silence is very obligeing as a mark of your puting a value on my correspondence that it has\n[[331.19]] no claim to: But the reason you give of your not haveing writ to me sooner has renew'd in me a\n[[331.20]] Sorrow which I could not but pertake in with all the world to whom were known (thō but by\n[[331.21]] Fame onely) the Great Endowments and Accomplishments of that Admirable Princess whose\n[[331.22]] Death has so much Afflicted you. One cannot have a deeper sense than I have of Your\n[[331.23]] Particular Loss in this Universal One: and if ever Greif was just yours must be allow'd to be so. </p>\n<p> [[331.24]] The Experience of the Wisest dos I find on some occasions attest to this truth, that <b><i>La\n[[331.25]] Sensibilité ne depend pas du Raisonnement</b></i>. There is however much difference betwixt\n[[331.26]] Them and Others; in that if Theire Reason dos not always Triumph, it yet at least hinders\n[[332.1]] Passion from doing so: and it is a Happy distinction not to be Led Captive by those Tyrants\n[[332.2]] under which the Generalitie of Mankind suffer a worse Slaverie than the most Arbitrarie\n[[332.3]] Masters can inflict. </p>\n<p> [[332.4]] I was Highly Honour'd in haveing any Thoughts of Mine Communicated to the Queene of\n[[332.5]] Prussia: but what you thought worth the answering I cannot wonder that She vouchsaf'd a\n[[332.6]] Heareing to. </p>\n<p> [[332.7]] I have flatter'd myself since my last to you that, possibly, within another yeare I might\n[[332.8]] have the oportunitie to converse with you better than by letters, in seeing You at Hanover: for if\n[[332.9]] some whose Judgments I rely upon, had thought it fit for a Young Gentleman destin'd to the\n[[332.10]] Study of the Law to spend a little Time first in Travelling, I should with his Fathers leave,\n[[332.11]] Gladly have accompany'd my Son in such a Voyage: both as proposing great Pleasure therein;\n[[332.12]] and also as haveing been persuaded that Travelling would contribute to my Health, which has\n[[332.13]] been much disorder'd this Last Yeare. </p>\n<p> [[332.14]] You have added to the many Reasons I have to Regret the Death of M^#'r Locke in leting me\n[[332.15]] know that it will hinder the Publick from Profiting by your Remarks upon his <i>Essay.</i> The\n[[332.16]] debates of Those who equally search Truth cannot but be advantageous to the discoverie of it,\n[[332.17]] or to the seting it in a Clearer Light: and which ever it be, both Parties in such cases will think\n[[332.18]] themselves alike Gainers. </p>\n<p> [[332.19]] I am sorry with you that the strength or weakness of any notion which you beleeve\n[[332.20]] important to be known, should not be so. It were to be wish'd that All who in respect of any\n[[332.21]] Usefull Truth, have views beyond others would not neglect to communicate them, that so They\n[[332.22]] may be out of danger of being useless to the World. This should methinks induce You to oblige,\n[[332.23]] as M^#'r Le Clerc tells you you would do, all the <k>Lovers of Philosophy</k>, in explaining at Large\n[[332.24]] Your Systeme; whereby it being set in a just Light, the advantages of it may neither be Lost; nor\n[[332.25]] prejudic'd, by the unskilfull representation that, it may be, some other Person who shall less\n[[332.26]] perfectly comprehend it, may some time, or other, venture to give of it. This I cannot but wish;\n[[332.27]] and This I find some hope you will be Excited to by M^#'r Bayle's Reflection upon Your\n[[332.28]] sentiments amongst Those of Others. </p>\n<p> [[333.1]] If you have read what has been writ betwixt him and M^#'r Le Clerc on the subject of the\n[[333.2]] Hypothesis of the Plastick Nature as asserted by my Father, I should be very glad to know\n[[333.3]] whether what M^#'r Bayle has offer'd dos amount to any thing more than a beging of the Question\n[[333.4]] That God cannot make an unknowing Agent so as to Act to wise Ends; yet without such his\n[[333.5]] Perpetual direction thereof as the Cartesian Hypothesis gives to Material Causes: for if it be\n[[333.6]] conceivable for God to do so, but not conceivable for senseless Matter of it self to Act for Ends;\n[[333.7]] My Fathers Hypothesis is methinks sufficiently secur'd from the <b>Retorsion of Atheists,</b>\n[[333.8]] without being <b>in the same Case</b> with any one which makes God the immediate Efficient\n[[333.9]] Cause of all the Effects of Nature. Since my Father dos not therein assert (as M^#'r Bayle says he\n[[333.10]] dos) That <b>God has been able to give to Creatures a Facultie of Produceing Excellent\n[[333.11]] Works, (viz such as is the Organization of Plants and Animals) seperate\n[[333.12]] from all Knowledge</b> etc: but Onely a Facultie of Executeing instrumentally his Ideas or\n[[333.13]] Designs in the Production of such <b>Excellent Works:</b> so that ( <b>according to him</b>) <b>there is</b>\n[[333.14]] (differently from what M^#'r Bayle asserts of his Hypothesis) <b>an inseparable union betwixt\n[[333.15]] the Power of produceing Excellent Works, and the Idea of theire Essence, and\n[[333.16]] manner of produceing them.</b> and it seems to me that there can be no Pretence for the\n[[333.17]] <b>Retorsion of Atheists</b> unless it were asserted, That God had been able to give to Creatures a\n[[333.18]] Facultie of produceing <b>excellent works,</b> the Ideas whereof never were in any Understanding:\n[[333.19]] But my Father is so far from asserting any such thing as This, that he holds the Operations of\n[[333.20]] the Plastick Nature to be <b>essentially and necessarilie dependent</b> on the Ideas in the\n[[333.21]] divine Intellect. So that (I conceive) if Matter could be suppos'd <b>to have of it self that same\n[[333.22]] Pow'r which Plastick Natures are said to have by the Gift of God,</b> it would not help\n[[334.1]] the Atheists Cause at all: Because the Pow'r giv'n to Plastick Natures being onely a Pow'r to\n[[334.2]] Execute the Ideas of a Perfect Mind; if there were no <b>Mind</b> in the Universe; this Pow'r in the\n[[334.3]] Matter must Lye for ever Dormant, and unproductive of any such Excellent Work as is spoken\n[[334.4]] of. </p>\n<p> [[334.5]] Thus I see not in my Fathers Hypothesis that there is Effectively (as M^#'r Bayle affirms) <b>a\n[[334.6]] Compatibilitie suppos'd of a Pow'r to Organize Animals, with the want of\n[[334.7]] Knowledge.</b> Since to the Production of such a work as This, two things (according to my\n[[334.8]] Father) must concur viz the Idea of the work to be Executed, with an Executive Pow'r of\n[[334.9]] bringing this Idea into real Existence: and it is onely the last of these which he ascribes to\n[[334.10]] Plastick Natures. </p>\n<p> [[334.11]] But M^#'r Bayle argues, That if Plastick Natures have no Intire Efficiencie of theire owne,\n[[334.12]] they will then need such <b>Perpetual direction</b> as is ascrib'd to Material Causes; and so my\n[[334.13]] Fathers <b>Hypothesis will be but in the same Case with the Cartesian.</b> M^#'r Bayle\n[[334.14]] Affirms this; but (as I think) he no where Proves it: All that he offers amounting (as I have\n[[334.15]] alreadie observ'd) onelie to a beging of this Question, that God cannot make a Creature to Act\n[[334.16]] but either <b>from Ideas</b> of what it dos; or else by such <b>Perpetual Direction</b> as that of the\n[[334.17]] Cartesian Material Causes. Whence he rigthly infers that if Plastick Natures Act not by Ideas\n[[334.18]] which They have, as it is presum'd the <b>Destroying Angel</b> did to <b>in distinguishing the\n[[334.19]] Egyptians from the Israelites</b> etc. They then must Act as He supposes <b>Fire</b>, had it so\n[[334.20]] pleas'd God, might have done, in Destroying the First Born of Egypt; Viz <b>by Gods Perpetual\n[[334.21]] applying and directing it.</b> </p>\n<p> [[334.22]] But to Any ones bare Assertion that a thing is Inconceivable, it is surely answer sufficient\n[[334.23]] that Others find it not to be so. Thō my Father has giv'n some instances which he thinks prove\n[[334.24]] the Possibilitie of such a manner of Action as he ascribes to Plastick Natures; Viz in the\n[[334.25]] Operations of <b>Habits</b>: as (for Example) Those of Singing and Danceing, which shall oftentimes\n[[334.26]] direct the Motions of the Body, or Voice, without any Consideration of what the next\n[[334.27]] Note, or Motion should be. </p>\n<p> [[334.28]] I beg Leave here to tresspass so much farther on Your Patience, as to observe to you one\n[[334.29]] thing wherin M^#'r Bayle is mistaken in reference to this matter, which has not been taken notice\n[[334.30]] of, and that is, in a presumeing that my Father design'd in introduceing his Hypothesis, to\n[[334.31]] oppose the Modern Cartesians: whereas He (not understanding French) did not know that the\n[[334.32]] Modern Cartesians differ'd so much from theire Master as to hold that God was the Immediate\n[[334.33]] Efficient Cause of all the Effects of Nature and the Hypothesis of the Plastick Nature (produc'd\n[[335.1]] by him for the acquiting from the suspicion of Atheism some who held a Plastick Life distinct\n[[335.2]] from the Animal) was very far from haveing the Cartesians in View; however They may find\n[[335.3]] themselves convinc'd when the opinion of such is condemn'd as held that God himself did All\n[[335.4]] <b>immediately</b> in the Efformation and <b>organization</b> of the Bodies of Animals, as well as the\n[[335.5]] other phenomena: which with the Opinion of all things comeing to pass Fortuitously, my Father\n[[335.6]] consider'd as the onely two Hypothesises which were oppos'd to that of Plastick Natures. </p>\n<p> [[335.7]] Now M^#'r Bayle considering <b>Perpetual Direction</b> onelie in such a sense as makes God\n[[335.8]] the Immediate Efficient cause of all the Effects of Nature, presumes that my Father is oblig'd to\n[[335.9]] deny all kind of Perpetual Direction to Plastick Natures; which (as I take it) he dos not: Since\n[[335.10]] (according to him) theire Operations are always determin'd by the Ideas in the Divine Intellect;\n[[335.11]] and his Hypothesis opposes onely such Direction as makes God the Immediate Efficient Cause\n[[335.12]] of all the Works of Nature. Plastick Natures being by him substituted as the Agents or\n[[335.13]] Executioners of the Divine Will and Pleasure. As <b>Perpetual Direction</b> then is understood, or\n[[335.14]] may be Explain'd, it seemes to me that Plastick Natures may be, and are by my Father, in what\n[[335.15]] he says of them, both affirm'd and deny'd to have <b>Perpetual Direction</b>: in which, if he talks\n[[335.16]] Contradictiously, or Inconsistently it lies upon M^#'r Bayle to show that he dos so, Stateing first\n[[335.17]] rightly what is herein asserted by him. </p>\n<p> [[335.18]] I pretend not at all to be Positive in any thing which I have here ventur'd to say on so nice\n[[335.19]] a subject, and so much above my Examination as not easilie to be set in a due Light by two so\n[[335.20]] Acute and extraordinarie Men as both M^#'r Le Clerc and M^#'r Bayle are. I onelie take the libertie\n[[335.21]] briefly to suggest to you my Thoughts thereon to the end that I may learn from you how far they\n[[335.22]] are Right. </p>\n<p> [[335.23]] The Last answer of M^#'r Le Clerc I have not yet read, his Civilitie in sending me one (which\n[[335.24]] I heare has miscarry'd) haveing made me thus Long neglect buying it. But I am told that M^#'r\n[[335.25]] Bayle will still reply: and that the subject of the Controversie is by some thought to be by M^#'r Le\n[[335.26]] Clercs answer too much enlarg'd in a Consideration of the usefulness of the Hypothesis of\n[[335.27]] Plastick Natures, which is not necessarie to the inquirie, whether, or no, this Hypothesis is\n[[335.28]] expos'd to the <b>Retorsion of Atheists.</b> </p>\n<p> [[335.29]] On which subject I have dwelt Long enough both to need your Pardon, and to forbid my\n[[335.30]] adding any thing More to this Letter that might induce you to explain to me farther your\n[[335.31]] Sentiments on some other important Inquiries mention'd in the last letter you Honourd me with. </p>\n<p> [[336.1]] I am with due acknowledgment of your Condescention in incourageing me in this Manner\n[[336.2]] to trouble you, and with very great Esteem and Respect </p>\n<p> [[336.3]] S^#'r Your Most Humble Servant Da Masham </p>\n<p> [[336.4]] Your last letter to me lay several weeks in this country before it came to my Hands as\n[[336.5]] being sent to a Gentlemans house where I was dayly exspected. All acidents of this nature will\n[[336.6]] be prevented and M^#'r Pickelaer have no farther trouble than to send them to the Post House if\n[[336.7]] you please to direct thus </p>\n<p> [[336.8]] For My Lady Masham at Oates. </p>\n<p> [[336.9]] To be left with M^#'r Harison at the Crowne in Harlow. </p>\n",
  "number": "109.",
  "title": "DAMARIS LADY MASHAM AN LEIBNIZ",
  "date": "Oates, 20. (31.) Oktober 1705. [105.111.]"
};
---

